  "method": {
    "classifier_boundary": "Ops send, resize, diff, reshape, mask, inference, collect per 16-frame batch, split evenly over the batch; read (video decoding) and retrieve (copy to CPU, hex encoding) excluded, as in p121.",
    "classifier_runs": "The comparison uses the sample-rate-1 run, which classifies every frame. p020 also ran the same classifier and tile size at every other sample rate on the same test videos; their mean cost per classified frame (classifier_runs, cost_across_runs) is the run-to-run spread under concurrent multi-GPU load, which is wider than the precision of any single ratio. Quote a rounded range (summary.ratio_of_means.all_runs), not one run's ratio. The operating point's run is the one p121 counts (checked: equal seconds and batch counts).",
    "detector_boundary": "Op detect per frame (batch size 1); read (video decoding) excluded.",
    "gpu_synchronization": "p020 times ops on the host (time.time_ns) without torch.cuda.synchronize. Its frames are pageable numpy arrays, and a host-to-device copy from pageable memory first synchronizes the stream, so send also waits for the previous batch's queued GPU work; the only other synchronization per batch is nonzero() in mask. inference and collect mostly launch kernels. host_interval_ms_per_frame_by_op is therefore host-side attribution under asynchronous execution and does not show where GPU time goes (for example, send against inference). Batch totals are unaffected except for the last batch of a video, whose GPU tail lands in retrieve (retrieve_ms_per_frame bounds it).",
    "hardware": {
